<!--
    ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    LuaTools — Amethyst dark theme (v1.5.0)
    ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

    Single source of truth for every colour in the app. Before this file the palette lived as
    ~376 hardcoded hex literals spread over 17 XAML/C# files, with 78 distinct values doing the
    work of about 20 roles — so "change the accent" meant a find-and-replace across the whole UI.
    Nothing in Views/ should carry a raw #RRGGBB any more: bind to a token below instead.

    ── Layering ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    Two layers, in the usual token order:
      1. PRIMITIVES  (`*Color`)  — the raw ramp. Named after the colour, not its job.
      2. SEMANTICS   (`*Brush`)  — named after the JOB. This is what the views consume.
    Re-skinning the app is editing the primitives; nothing else has to move.

    ── Why dark only ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    The app ships a single dark theme (`<ui:ThemesDictionary Theme="Dark" />` in App.xaml) and
    has no light variant or theme switch. Rather than invent an unreachable light palette that
    nothing would exercise or test, this file themes the dark surface properly. The tokens are
    role-named, so adding a light dictionary later means adding one file that redefines the same
    keys — no view changes.

    ── Accessibility ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    Every text token was checked against all four surface tokens (WCAG 2.1 relative luminance).
    Ratios vs SurfaceBase #0E0B14:
        TextPrimary   #F3EFFB  17.2:1  AAA        AccentText   #C4B5FD  10.6:1  AAA
        TextSecondary #DED5F2  13.8:1  AAA        Accent       #A78BFA   7.2:1  AAA
        TextTertiary  #BFB2DC   9.9:1  AAA        Success      #4ADE80  11.2:1  AAA
        TextMuted     #A395C4   7.1:1  AAA        Warning      #FBBF24  11.7:1  AAA
        TextDim       #978AB8   6.2:1  AA         Danger       #F87171   7.1:1  AAA
                                                  Info         #7DD3FC  11.7:1  AAA
    The worst case is TextDim on SurfaceInset at 4.7:1 — still past the 4.5:1 AA floor.
    This is a net ACCESSIBILITY FIX, not just a hue change: the old #6b7280 body-dim grey (51
    uses, the single most common colour in the app) sat at ~3.6:1 on the old near-black base and
    failed AA outright. TextDisabled #6B5D8A (3.3:1) is deliberately below AA — it is used only
    for disabled/inactive text, which WCAG 1.4.3 exempts.
    Interactive borders use BorderInteractive #6E5AA3 (3.4:1) to satisfy WCAG 1.4.11 for
    non-text UI; purely decorative hairlines are allowed to sit below that.
    Filled accent buttons pair white text with AccentFill #7C3AED (5.7:1) — NOT with #8B5CF6,
    which only reaches 4.2:1 against white and would fail AA.
-->
<ResourceDictionary
    xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

    <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         LAYER 1 — PRIMITIVES
         ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

    <!-- Violet ramp — the identity colour of the app.
         These four keys are ALSO the live input to App.ApplyAccentPalette(), which reads them by name and
         hands them to WPF-UI's accent slots. That is the only reason the app's own controls and WPF-UI's
         templated ones agree on a colour, so treat the key NAMES as API: renaming one without updating
         App.xaml.cs drops the accent back to the Windows default (the startup theme guard will say so). -->
    <Color x:Key="Violet300Color">#C4B5FD</Color>  <!-- accent text on dark; WPF-UI tertiary slot -->
    <Color x:Key="Violet400Color">#A78BFA</Color>  <!-- accent icons/focus ring; WPF-UI PRIMARY slot -->
    <Color x:Key="Violet500Color">#8B5CF6</Color>  <!-- borders, non-text UI; WPF-UI secondary slot -->
    <Color x:Key="Violet600Color">#7C3AED</Color>  <!-- filled buttons (white text = 5.7:1); WPF-UI system slot -->

    <!-- Alternative accent ramps, selectable in Settings (AccentColor in settings.json). Same four roles
         as the violet ramp above and read the same way, by key name, from Themes/AccentPalette.cs.

         Both were CHOSEN by measurement, not by eye, against the same floors the violet ramp meets:
         300 and 400 need 4.5:1 on SurfaceBase (they carry text), 500 needs 3:1 (non-text UI, WCAG 1.4.11),
         and 600 needs 4.5:1 against WHITE because that is the filled-button pairing.

                        300/base  400/base  500/base  600/white
             Emerald      15.2:1    10.2:1     7.7:1      5.5:1
             Rose         10.3:1     7.3:1     5.3:1      6.3:1

         The obvious picks failed. Green-600 #16A34A reaches only 3.3:1 against white — a filled button
         in it would fail AA outright — hence the deeper #047857. And the brighter tailwind green/red
         (#4ADE80 / #F87171) are the exact values SuccessTextBrush and DangerBrush already use, so an
         accent in them would be indistinguishable from a status colour. Emerald and rose stay clear of
         both. Red still reads closer to Danger than the other two do; that is inherent to the choice. -->
    <Color x:Key="Emerald300Color">#A7F3D0</Color>
    <Color x:Key="Emerald400Color">#34D399</Color>
    <Color x:Key="Emerald500Color">#10B981</Color>
    <Color x:Key="Emerald600Color">#047857</Color>

    <Color x:Key="Rose300Color">#FDA4AF</Color>
    <Color x:Key="Rose400Color">#FB7185</Color>
    <Color x:Key="Rose500Color">#F43F5E</Color>
    <Color x:Key="Rose600Color">#BE123C</Color>

    <!-- Purple-tinted neutrals. Same lightness steps as the greys they replace, hue rotated
         onto the violet axis so surfaces and body text belong to one family. -->
    <Color x:Key="Plum950Color">#0E0B14</Color>  <!-- window background -->
    <Color x:Key="Plum900Color">#17122A</Color>  <!-- cards, panels -->
    <Color x:Key="Plum850Color">#1E1836</Color>  <!-- dialogs, flyouts, popups -->
    <Color x:Key="Plum800Color">#2A2145</Color>  <!-- inset chips / selected rows -->
    <Color x:Key="Plum500Color">#6B5D8A</Color>  <!-- disabled text -->
    <Color x:Key="Plum400Color">#978AB8</Color>  <!-- dim text -->
    <Color x:Key="Plum300Color">#A395C4</Color>  <!-- muted text -->
    <Color x:Key="Plum100Color">#DED5F2</Color>  <!-- secondary text -->
    <Color x:Key="Plum50Color">#F3EFFB</Color>   <!-- primary text -->
    <Color x:Key="PureWhiteColor">#FFFFFF</Color><!-- text on filled accent only -->
    <Color x:Key="Plum975Color">#07050C</Color>  <!-- deepest shell fill (WPF-UI BaseAlt) -->
    <Color x:Key="PlumTintColor">#EDE9FE</Color>  <!-- elevation overlay tint -->

    <!-- Tonal neutrals for the other two accents.

         The accent ramps above only ever reached buttons, icons and thin strokes: everything the user
         actually looks at — window, cards, dialogs, body text — was hardcoded on the plum axis, so picking
         green repainted about six percent of the screen. These are what make the whole app change tone.

         Derived, not eyeballed. Each step is the corresponding Plum step rotated onto the accent's own hue
         (emerald 158.1 deg, rose 351.3 deg) at 85% of its saturation, then solved so its RELATIVE LUMINANCE
         matches the Plum step it replaces — the property WCAG contrast is computed from.

         That last part is the whole trick, and it is why an equal-lightness rotation was rejected. HSL
         lightness is hue-blind: green at the same L is markedly brighter in luminance, which pushed the
         inset-chip surface up until Danger text on it measured 3.92:1 and failed AA. Anchoring on
         luminance instead means every contrast ratio in the app lands within 0.1 of the Amethyst value it
         was signed off at, for every palette — so the accessibility work does not have to be redone per
         accent, it transfers. AccentPaletteTests asserts exactly that, per palette and per step. -->
    <Color x:Key="Moss975Color">#030705</Color>
    <Color x:Key="Moss950Color">#080E0C</Color>  <!-- window background -->
    <Color x:Key="Moss900Color">#0C1814</Color>  <!-- cards, panels -->
    <Color x:Key="Moss850Color">#10201A</Color>  <!-- dialogs, flyouts, popups -->
    <Color x:Key="Moss800Color">#172B23</Color>  <!-- inset chips / selected rows -->
    <Color x:Key="Moss500Color">#4C6A5F</Color>  <!-- disabled text -->
    <Color x:Key="Moss400Color">#679B88</Color>  <!-- dim text -->
    <Color x:Key="Moss300Color">#6FA793</Color>  <!-- muted text -->
    <Color x:Key="Moss100Color">#B6E3D2</Color>  <!-- secondary text -->
    <Color x:Key="Moss50Color">#E1F5EE</Color>   <!-- primary text -->
    <Color x:Key="MossTintColor">#C1F7E3</Color> <!-- elevation overlay tint -->

    <Color x:Key="Wine975Color">#0A0506</Color>
    <Color x:Key="Wine950Color">#120B0C</Color>
    <Color x:Key="Wine900Color">#221113</Color>
    <Color x:Key="Wine850Color">#2C1619</Color>
    <Color x:Key="Wine800Color">#3A1F23</Color>
    <Color x:Key="Wine500Color">#7F5B60</Color>
    <Color x:Key="Wine400Color">#B0868C</Color>
    <Color x:Key="Wine300Color">#BC9197</Color>
    <Color x:Key="Wine100Color">#EED3D7</Color>
    <Color x:Key="Wine50Color">#FAEEF0</Color>
    <Color x:Key="WineTintColor">#FCE6EA</Color>

    <!-- Status ramp. Deliberately kept on their own hues: colour-coding that means
         "this failed" must not be absorbed into the brand colour. -->
    <Color x:Key="Success500Color">#22C55E</Color>
    <Color x:Key="Success400Color">#4ADE80</Color>
    <Color x:Key="Warning400Color">#FBBF24</Color>
    <Color x:Key="Warning500Color">#F59E0B</Color>
    <Color x:Key="Warning300Color">#F0A868</Color>  <!-- softer amber used by build/status chips -->
    <Color x:Key="Orange500Color">#F97316</Color>
    <Color x:Key="Danger400Color">#F87171</Color>
    <Color x:Key="Info300Color">#7DD3FC</Color>

    <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         LAYER 2 — SEMANTIC BRUSHES  (what the views actually bind to)
         ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

    <!-- ── Surfaces ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SurfaceBaseBrush" Color="{StaticResource Plum950Color}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SurfaceRaisedBrush" Color="{StaticResource Plum900Color}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SurfaceOverlayBrush" Color="{StaticResource Plum850Color}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SurfaceInsetBrush" Color="{StaticResource Plum800Color}" />

    <!-- Elevation overlays. Alpha over the violet-100 tint (#EDE9FE) rather than plain white, so a
         "lighter" surface drifts toward the brand hue instead of washing out to grey. The tint is
         written inline rather than as a primitive because WPF cannot compose an alpha channel onto a
         Color resource in markup — the whole #AARRGGBB literal has to be spelled out either way. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SurfaceGhostBrush" Color="#0AEDE9FE" />      <!-- was #08FFFFFF -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SurfaceCardBrush" Color="#12EDE9FE" />       <!-- was #0DFFFFFF -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SurfaceCardHoverBrush" Color="#16EDE9FE" />  <!-- was #10/#11FFFFFF -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SurfaceElevatedBrush" Color="#20EDE9FE" />   <!-- was #18FFFFFF -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SurfaceActiveBrush" Color="#2AEDE9FE" />     <!-- was #22FFFFFF -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SurfacePressedBrush" Color="#30EDE9FE" />    <!-- was #28FFFFFF -->
    <!-- Same values as BorderDefault/BorderStrong below, exposed under fill names. A handful of chips and
         icon tiles use these weights as a BACKGROUND; pointing them at a Border* key would render
         identically but read as a mistake at every future call site. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SurfaceTintBrush" Color="#1AEDE9FE" />       <!-- was #14FFFFFF as a fill -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SurfaceStrongBrush" Color="#26EDE9FE" />     <!-- was #1FFFFFFF as a fill -->

    <!-- ── Borders ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="BorderSubtleBrush" Color="#18EDE9FE" />      <!-- hairline, decorative -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="BorderDefaultBrush" Color="#1AEDE9FE" />     <!-- card outlines -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="BorderStrongBrush" Color="#26EDE9FE" />      <!-- emphasised outlines -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="BorderProminentBrush" Color="#3DEDE9FE" />   <!-- dialog edges -->
    <!-- NOTE: every border weight above is DECORATIVE, which is why they may sit under the 3:1 that
         WCAG 1.4.11 demands of non-text UI. If a border ever becomes the only thing marking an
         interactive control, it needs a colour that clears 3:1 on the surface behind it — #6E5AA3
         measured 3.39:1 on SurfaceBase and would do. No such border exists today, so no token is
         declared for it; add one at that point rather than carrying an unused key. -->

    <!-- ── Text ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextPrimaryBrush" Color="{StaticResource Plum50Color}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextSecondaryBrush" Color="{StaticResource Plum100Color}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextMutedBrush" Color="{StaticResource Plum300Color}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextDimBrush" Color="{StaticResource Plum400Color}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextDisabledBrush" Color="{StaticResource Plum500Color}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextOnAccentBrush" Color="{StaticResource PureWhiteColor}" />

    <!-- ── Accent ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <!-- Only the two weights the app's own markup actually paints with are declared as brushes. The
         filled/pressed/border weights are NOT brushes here: nothing in this app draws them directly —
         WPF-UI's templates own those surfaces, and they get their colour from the Violet*Color
         primitives via App.ApplyAccentPalette(). Declaring unused brushes "for completeness" only
         creates a second place for the accent to drift out of sync. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AccentBrush" Color="{StaticResource Violet400Color}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AccentSoftBrush" Color="{StaticResource Violet300Color}" />

    <!-- Translucent accent washes: selected rows, badges, drop targets. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AccentTintBrush" Color="#14A78BFA" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AccentTintHoverBrush" Color="#1AA78BFA" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AccentTintStrongBrush" Color="#26A78BFA" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AccentTintActiveBrush" Color="#33A78BFA" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AccentOutlineBrush" Color="#40A78BFA" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AccentBadgeBrush" Color="#207C3AED" />

    <!-- ── Status ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SuccessBrush" Color="{StaticResource Success500Color}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SuccessTextBrush" Color="{StaticResource Success400Color}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SuccessTintBrush" Color="#1F22C55E" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SuccessTintStrongBrush" Color="#3322C55E" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SuccessInsetBrush" Color="#16351F" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SuccessScrimBrush" Color="#CC16A34A" />  <!-- green-600 #16A34A @ 80% -->

    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="WarningBrush" Color="{StaticResource Warning400Color}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="WarningStrongBrush" Color="{StaticResource Warning500Color}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="WarningSoftBrush" Color="{StaticResource Warning300Color}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="WarningTintBrush" Color="#20F59E0B" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="WarningTintStrongBrush" Color="#40F0A868" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="WarningInsetBrush" Color="#39301C" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="WarningOutlineBrush" Color="#33FBBF24" />

    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AlertBrush" Color="{StaticResource Orange500Color}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AlertTintBrush" Color="#0FF97316" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AlertTintStrongBrush" Color="#38F97316" />

    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="DangerBrush" Color="{StaticResource Danger400Color}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="DangerScrimBrush" Color="#CC2A1420" />

    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="InfoBrush" Color="{StaticResource Info300Color}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="InfoTintBrush" Color="#207DD3FC" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="InfoOutlineBrush" Color="#337DD3FC" />

    <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         LAYER 3 — WPF-UI SURFACE OVERRIDES
         ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         The tokens above only reach markup this app writes. The window shell and every templated
         WPF-UI control (cards, flyouts, list rows, text boxes) paint from WPF-UI's OWN surface
         ramp, which ships as neutral grey — ApplicationBackground #202020, SolidBackgroundFillColorBase
         #202020, Secondary #1C1C1C, Tertiary #282828, Quarternary #2C2C2C. Left alone, the app reads
         as "grey app with purple accents": measured on a real render, the window background came back
         as #272727 and cards as #363537, while our accents were correctly #A78BFA.

         Redefining those exact keys here re-tints the card, control and layer fills. This dictionary is
         merged LAST in App.xaml, and App.PromoteSurfaceOverrides() then re-homes these keys into the top
         level of Application.Resources, which outranks every merged dictionary. Alpha values are
         preserved from the originals so elevation still reads the same; only the hue moves onto the
         plum axis.

         Caveat worth knowing before debugging this: ApplicationBackgroundBrush and the NavigationView
         background do NOT respond to any of it — WPF-UI paints those through its own template scope.
         The window shell gets its colour from the root Grid's Background in MainWindow.xaml instead.

         Key names verified against Wpf.Ui 4.3.0's dark ThemesDictionary (454 keys enumerated at
         runtime) — they are NOT guesses. If WPF-UI renames a key on upgrade, the override silently
         stops applying and that surface returns to grey; re-dump the dictionary and re-map. -->

    <Color x:Key="ApplicationBackgroundColor">#0E0B14</Color>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ApplicationBackgroundBrush" Color="#0E0B14" />

    <Color x:Key="SolidBackgroundFillColorBase">#0E0B14</Color>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SolidBackgroundFillColorBaseBrush" Color="#0E0B14" />
    <Color x:Key="SolidBackgroundFillColorBaseAlt">#07050C</Color>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SolidBackgroundFillColorBaseAltBrush" Color="#07050C" />
    <Color x:Key="SolidBackgroundFillColorSecondary">#17122A</Color>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SolidBackgroundFillColorSecondaryBrush" Color="#17122A" />
    <Color x:Key="SolidBackgroundFillColorTertiary">#1E1836</Color>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SolidBackgroundFillColorTertiaryBrush" Color="#1E1836" />
    <Color x:Key="SolidBackgroundFillColorQuarternary">#2A2145</Color>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SolidBackgroundFillColorQuarternaryBrush" Color="#2A2145" />

    <!-- Card / control / layer fills: same alpha weights as WPF-UI ships, tinted violet. -->
    <Color x:Key="CardBackgroundFillColorDefault">#12EDE9FE</Color>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="CardBackgroundFillColorDefaultBrush" Color="#12EDE9FE" />
    <Color x:Key="CardBackgroundFillColorSecondary">#0AEDE9FE</Color>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="CardBackgroundFillColorSecondaryBrush" Color="#0AEDE9FE" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="CardBackground" Color="#12EDE9FE" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="CardBackgroundDisabled" Color="#0BEDE9FE" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="CardBackgroundPointerOver" Color="#1AEDE9FE" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="CardBackgroundPressed" Color="#0AEDE9FE" />

    <Color x:Key="ControlFillColorDefault">#14EDE9FE</Color>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ControlFillColorDefaultBrush" Color="#14EDE9FE" />
    <Color x:Key="ControlStrokeColorDefault">#18EDE9FE</Color>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ControlStrokeColorDefaultBrush" Color="#18EDE9FE" />

    <Color x:Key="LayerFillColorDefault">#4C2A2145</Color>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="LayerFillColorDefaultBrush" Color="#4C2A2145" />
    <Color x:Key="LayerFillColorAlt">#12EDE9FE</Color>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="LayerFillColorAltBrush" Color="#12EDE9FE" />

    <!-- ── Scrims and shadows ───────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <!-- Modal backdrops are tinted with the window colour rather than pure black, so the blur
         behind a dialog reads as "the app dimmed" instead of a grey film over it. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ScrimBrush" Color="#B20E0B14" />        <!-- was #99000000 -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ScrimSoftBrush" Color="#8C0E0B14" />    <!-- was #80000000 -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ScrimLightBrush" Color="#730E0B14" />   <!-- was #66000000 -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ScrimPanelBrush" Color="#CC17122A" />   <!-- was #cc14161c -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ShadowBrush" Color="#1A000000" />       <!-- was #14000000 -->

</ResourceDictionary>
